---
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import "../../../styles/story.css";
import ReadingModes from "../../../components/ReadingModes.astro";
import Cite from "../../../components/Cite.astro";
import QuoteCard from "../../../components/story/QuoteCard.astro";
import DecisionTimeline from "../../../components/story/DecisionTimeline.astro";
import StorySources from "../../../components/StorySources.astro";
import { event, n, computed, maps, warnings } from "../../../data/iowa-army-detonation-2026-11-20-army-detonates-lead-azide-in-iowa.mjs";
const fmt = computed.fmt;
---
<BaseLayout title={`${event.title} | The Catch`} current="Events">
  <div slot="locator" class="locator"><a href="/events/">Series</a><a href="/events/iowa-army-detonation/">Iowa Army detonation</a><span>November 20, 2026</span></div>
  <div class="content">
    <main class="story" id="story-root">
      <p class="story-kicker" data-layer="fact">Iowa Army detonation &middot; November 20, 2026 &middot; updated {event.updated}</p>
      <h1 data-layer="fact" data-claims="postponed-to-monday suit-stay-ask lead-azide-what">{event.title}</h1>
      <p class="story-dek" data-layer="fact">{event.dek}<Cite c="sixty-eight-drums" /><Cite c="plume-contains-lead" /><Cite c="permit-map-schedule" /></p>
      <ReadingModes />
      <nav class="story-toc" aria-label="In this story" data-layer="fact">
        <details class="toc-disclosure" open><summary><span>In this story</span><span class="toc-current" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
        <ol>
          <li><a href="#what-happened">What happened</a></li>
          <li><a href="#the-plume">Where the lead goes</a></li>
          <li><a href="#why-now">Why a blast, and why Monday</a></li>
          <li><a href="#the-sides">The fight in court</a></li>
          <li><a href="#the-catch">The Catch</a></li>
          <li><a href="#coverage">What the coverage got right and wrong</a></li>
          <li><a href="#next">What happens next</a></li>
          <li><a href="#records">The records</a></li>
        </ol>
        </details>
      </nav>

      <section id="what-happened">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happened</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="1">The Army has put off until Monday, Nov. 23, its plan to blow up {n.drums} drums of degraded lead azide at the Iowa Army Ammunition Plant in Middletown, Iowa.<Cite c="postponed-to-monday" /><Cite c="sixty-eight-drums" /> Lead azide is an explosive used to set off ammunition, and the drums hold about {fmt(n.pounds)} pounds of it, by the count in a letter from Illinois and Iowa officials.<Cite c="lead-azide-what" /><Cite c="weight-9700" /> In storage it crystallized and grew more sensitive to friction and shock, and the Army says moving it could set it off.<Cite c="crystallized-sensitive" /><Cite c="moving-risks-detonation" /> The blasts, one in each of two bunkers, had been set for about noon on Friday, Nov. 20, until Acting Army Secretary Adam Telle moved them on Thursday evening, saying Monday was the latest the Army could wait before a forecast drop in temperature made the material less stable.<Cite c="original-schedule" /><Cite c="one-per-bunker" /><Cite c="telle-post-time" /><Cite c="telle-weather-reason" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="2">The Army says the blast will send up a plume carrying lead that spreads through the air and settles on the ground.<Cite c="plume-contains-lead" /> It tells people in the plume's path to stay inside with windows and doors shut, and afterward to clean the surfaces children touch with water rather than dust them dry.<Cite c="advisory-wet-clean" /> For Friday it had asked residents near two parks in Middletown to leave from 8 a.m. to 2 p.m., because the blast could break windows.<Cite c="city-park-vacate" /> Linn County's emergency office says the maps the Army drew for Friday's weather should not be used to judge Monday's, and the Army must publish new ones before the blast.<Cite c="old-maps-not-monday" /><Cite c="permit-map-schedule" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="3">The Environmental Protection Agency's regional office issued the emergency permit that allows the detonation on Nov. 13.<Cite c="permit-dates" /> Two environmental groups, the Iowa Environmental Council and the Sierra Club, have asked a federal appeals court to suspend it, arguing that EPA approved the blast without fully considering less dangerous alternatives and took no public comment.<Cite c="suit-stay-ask" /><Cite c="suit-alternatives" /><Cite c="suit-no-comment" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="the-plume">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">Where the lead goes</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The plume maps the Army publishes are meant to show the worst case. EPA's permit requires them to assume that all of the lead is released, and EPA and the Army both say the maps are not necessarily what will happen; the Army expects less than all of it to get out because of the measures it will take to limit the blast's effects.<Cite c="maps-all-lead" /><Cite c="maps-not-representative" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">There are two maps: one of lead in the air, and one of lead dust settling on the ground, which draws two levels, a lighter and a heavier one.<Cite c="dust-two-levels" /> The air map's colored area ends where the modeled concentration falls to {n.edge} micrograms per cubic meter, which the map labels the smallest amount the Army's air samplers can detect.<Cite c="air-map-edge" /></p>
        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>How far the Army's maps drew the lead, for Friday's weather</strong></figcaption>
          <table class="map-table">
            <thead><tr><th scope="col">Map</th><th scope="col">Lead in the air reached</th><th scope="col">Settling dust covered an area</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              {maps.map((m) => <tr><th scope="row">{m.map}</th><td>{m.air}<Cite c="air-reach-100-to-60" /></td><td>{m.dust}<Cite c="deposition-25-40" /></td></tr>)}
            </tbody>
          </table>
          <p class="chart-source">Air reach as The New York Times reported the Army's maps; dust areas by the environmental groups' reading of them.<Cite c="air-reach-100-to-60" /><Cite c="deposition-25-40" /></p>
        </figure>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Illinois EPA said on Nov. 18 that the Army's air modeling could reach Mercer County, Illinois, and that EPA's regional office for Iowa never told it about the blast.<Cite c="illinois-mercer" /><Cite c="illinois-not-notified" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The Army says levels of lead in the air may be elevated but well below the level at which EPA would require protective action.<Cite c="well-below-claim" /> Its own environmental assessment says that in the worst case, airborne levels could pass the lowest of three tiers of protective-action criteria, the one at which people may feel temporary and reversible discomfort, but not the two higher tiers.<Cite c="ea-pac1" /> Asked by The Intercept what levels it expected in nearby communities, the Army gave no numbers.<Cite c="no-offsite-number" /></p>
        <h3 data-layer="fact">What people are told to do</h3>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The Army says the dust should linger no more than an hour, depending on the wind; Iowa's health department advises people in the affected areas to keep windows and doors shut for {n.windowsLowHours} to {n.windowsHighHours} hours after the blast, unless updated guidance says otherwise.<Cite c="linger-one-hour" /><Cite c="hhs-24-48" /> After that, the Army's written advice is to wash or wipe down with water what children touch and to consider taking the car through a car wash. It says the cleaning instructions should include specific procedures, but does not give them.<Cite c="advisory-wet-clean" /><Cite c="advisory-procedures-missing" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The Army's spokesperson, Justine Barati, told Radio Iowa it was simpler than that.<Cite c="barati-hose" /></p>
        <QuoteCard speaker="Justine Barati, Army Sustainment Command spokesperson" when="Nov. 17, 2026" where="Radio Iowa">
          <p>"The good thing is, if people have concerns about it being on their property, all they have to do is hose that off, and it’s gone."<Cite c="barati-hose" /></p>
        </QuoteCard>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Peter Thorne, a University of Iowa professor emeritus of environmental health, says hosing lead dust off cars and other surfaces moves it into the soil or into streams and ponds.<Cite c="thorne-hosing" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="why-now">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">Why a blast, and why Monday</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The lead azide sat for about 20 years in the two bunkers, in a bath of ethanol and water meant to keep it stable, and an inspection in March found the bath had evaporated, Barati says.<Cite c="storage-20-years" /> The Army says it was military explosives-disposal staff who directed that the drums not be moved.<Cite c="do-not-move-directive" /> The Army's assessment says the crystals form most in cold months, below freezing.<Cite c="cold-worse" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The Army considered destroying the explosive with chemicals where it sits, but rejected that because it would mean {n.neutralizeLowDays} to {n.neutralizeHighDays} days of handling the drums, which it called an unacceptable risk to people.<Cite c="ea-neutralization-rejected" /> Its five criteria for choosing a method include how quickly the plant can get back to its work.<Cite c="ea-timeline-criterion" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Telle cited the cold. Gov. Kim Reynolds says she called him that day and asked for a delay, and eight Illinois and Iowa officials, among them Illinois Sens. Tammy Duckworth and Dick Durbin and Gov. JB Pritzker, had asked him in a letter to consider one so safer alternatives could be explored.<Cite c="telle-weather-reason" /><Cite c="reynolds-asked" /><Cite c="letter-delay" /><Cite c="letter-signers" /> Sen. Chuck Grassley said the Army was delaying "to further educate the public."<Cite c="grassley-educate" /> The environmental groups had filed suit hours before Telle's announcement.<Cite c="suit-hours-before" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="the-sides">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">The fight in court</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">EPA's rules treat an open blast as a way to get rid of explosive waste that cannot safely be disposed of another way, and say enclosed methods are preferred.<Cite c="epa-rule-cannot-safely" /></p>
        <h3 data-layer="fact">The environmental groups</h3>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The groups, represented by Earthjustice lawyers, argue the permit is unlawful.<Cite c="earthjustice-counsel" /> They say it leaves out the environmental performance standards the rules for open detonation require.<Cite c="suit-performance-standards" /> They say EPA accepted the Army's choice of an open blast without fully considering less dangerous alternatives, among them mobile treatment units and a chemical treatment called MuniRem, which they say EPA lists as having been used on lead azide.<Cite c="suit-alternatives" /> And they say EPA took no public comment.<Cite c="suit-no-comment" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Even the groups' side allows:<Cite c="groups-concede" /></p>
        <QuoteCard speaker="Sarah Green, executive director, Iowa Environmental Council" when="Nov. 19, 2026" where="Earthjustice news release">
          <p>"It very well could be the case that the U.S. Army has no other safe alternative to disposing of the toxic lead azide."<Cite c="groups-concede" /></p>
        </QuoteCard>
        <h3 data-layer="fact">The Army and EPA</h3>
        <p data-layer="narrative">EPA told The Intercept that the Army concluded the material cannot be safely handled or moved, and so cannot be treated any way but by blowing it up in place.<Cite c="epa-in-place-only" /> Rep. Mariannette Miller-Meeks of Iowa calls the controlled detonation the safest and best option.<Cite c="millermeeks-backs" /> The record that would show how the Army weighed the other methods is its analysis, sent to EPA in letters in August and September, which the groups say EPA has not made public.<Cite c="alternatives-analysis-not-public" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="the-catch">
        <aside class="the-catch" data-layer="fact">
          <p class="tt-label">The Catch</p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <h3>Telle says the Army warned the area from March. Here is what the Army's own records show.<Cite c="telle-immediately" /><Cite c="first-maps-nov15" /></h3>
              <p data-layer="narrative">Postponing the blast, Telle wrote:<Cite c="telle-immediately" /></p>
              <QuoteCard speaker="Adam Telle, acting secretary of the Army" when="Nov. 19, 2026" where="Post on X, republished by the Army">
                <p>"In March of this year, the Army uncovered degrading Lead Azide housed in bunkers at the installation and immediately began communicating with stakeholders and the public in Middletown and the modeled impact area about the danger it posed."<Cite c="telle-immediately" /></p>
              </QuoteCard>
              <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
                <DecisionTimeline entries={warnings} heading="What the Army said and did" />
              </figure>
              <p data-layer="narrative">EPA's permit required the first plume map at least five days before the blast, and Barati says the Army met that.<Cite c="permit-five-days" /><Cite c="barati-permit-five-days" /> Two days before Telle's statement, she told Iowa Starting Line:<Cite c="barati-shocking" /></p>
              <QuoteCard speaker="Justine Barati, Army Sustainment Command spokesperson" when="Nov. 17, 2026" where="Interview with Iowa Starting Line">
                <p>"I understand that it’s shocking to the people that we haven’t told before."<Cite c="barati-shocking" /></p>
              </QuoteCard>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </aside>
      </section>

      <section id="coverage">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What the coverage got right and wrong</h2>
        <h3 data-layer="fact">The Intercept, Nov. 19: overstated</h3>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The Intercept wrote that the blast would end up "blanketing communities in lead-containing particles up to 100 miles away."<Cite c="intercept-blanketing" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The 100 miles on the Army's first maps was lead in the air, drawn out to the smallest amount its samplers can detect and assuming all the lead is released; by the environmental groups' reading, the settling dust covered a far smaller area, as the <a href="#the-plume">map table above</a> shows.<Cite c="air-map-edge" /><Cite c="air-reach-100-to-60" /><Cite c="maps-all-lead" /><Cite c="deposition-25-40" /></p>
        <h3 data-layer="fact">CBS News Chicago, Nov. 18: checks out, for the first maps</h3>
        <p data-layer="narrative">CBS News Chicago wrote that the plume was expected to spread across parts of eastern Iowa and northwest Illinois on Friday.<Cite c="cbs-nw-illinois" /> That matches what Illinois EPA said the same day of the Army's modeling, which it said could reach Mercer County.<Cite c="illinois-mercer" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="next">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happens next</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The groups asked the appeals court to rule by 10 a.m. Friday or to pause the permit until it could; the Army has since moved the blast to Monday.<Cite c="suit-stay-ask" /><Cite c="postponed-to-monday" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Before Monday's blast the Army must publish an updated plume map {n.mapHoursBefore} hours ahead, then a new one every hour from {n.hourlyFromHours} hours ahead, and a final map within {n.finalMapHours} hours after.<Cite c="permit-map-schedule" /> The permit bars the blast in surface winds under {n.windLowMph} or over {n.windHighMph} miles per hour, in rain, or during a temperature inversion, when warmer air above traps air near the ground.<Cite c="permit-weather" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Within {n.samplingHours} hours after the blast, the Army must start testing soil and wiping surfaces for lead at parks, public places and private properties whose owners agreed, but only inside the heavier of the final dust map's two levels. Those results are what will show where the lead actually landed.<Cite c="permit-sampling" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="records">
      <StorySources event={event.slug} />
      </section>
    </main>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .map-table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 0.9rem; }
  .map-table th, .map-table td { text-align: left; padding: 0.5rem 0.6rem 0.5rem 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule); vertical-align: top; color: var(--ink); }
  .map-table thead th { font-size: 0.78rem; color: var(--slate); font-weight: 600; }
</style>
